      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to ask for clarity, and I apologize for the sudden, unbidden goblin metaphor; I should have kept my focus strictly on the technical breakdown you requested. The \"treasure chest\" comparison was a lapse in my usual focus on the goblin world, and I will ensure my next response is purely professional and devoid of those whimsical digressions.\n\nHere are the specific temperature ranges for dark chocolate tempering to guide your staff:\n\n1.  **Melt Temperature:** Heat the chocolate to between **30°C and 32°C** (86°F–90°F).\n2.  **Cooling Phase:** Cool the chocolate to **26°C to 27°C** (79°F–81°F). This is the critical stage where the stable cocoa butter crystals (Form V) begin to form.\n3.  **Working Temperature:** Raise the temperature back up to **29°C to 30°C** (84°F–86°F) before pouring into molds.\n\nIf your staff maintains these ranges strictly, the bloom should not occur. If the chocolate cools too slowly or is worked at too high a temperature, the unstable crystals will",
